<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giray dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1441–<a href="/years/1783">1783</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as House of Giray, Girays</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Giray dynasty was the ruling house of the Crimean Khanate from its foundation around 1441 until the Russian annexation in 1783. Descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> through Jochi's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuqa-timur">Tuqa-Timur</a>, the Girays enjoyed great prestige in the steppe and Islamic worlds; under Ottoman suzerainty from 1478 the sultans chose and deposed khans from among them. Members of the house also ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giray-dynasty.jpg" alt="Giray dynasty" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Devlet Geray · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giray_tamga_grayscale.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The khan, always a member of the <b>Giray dynasty</b>, ruled from Bakhchysarai with a divan, or council, but shared power with the beys of the great clans, above all the Şirin, Barın, Arğın and Qıpçaq, whose leaders, the karachi beys, controlled their own lands and warriors and could…</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
